<!--
    A call loses every argument when its callee forwards them to a target
    through a call that also passes stack arguments.

        int getopt_long(int argc, char **argv, const char *opts, const void *lo, int *ix)
        { return internal(argc, argv, opts, lo, ix, 0, 0); }
        int internal(int argc, char **argv, const char *opts, const void *lo,
                     int *ix, int long_only, int posix)
        { ...; int r = internal_r(argc, argv, opts, lo, ix, long_only, &amp;gdata, posix); ...; }

    gcc 11.4 -O2 (-fno-builtin -fno-stack-protector -fcf-protection=none
    -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables), linked -static -nostdlib -Ttext=0x403000
    -Tdata=0x405000; only .text is here.  It is tar's gnulib getopt shape:
    getopt_long is `sub $0x10,%rsp; xor %r9d,%r9d; push $0; call internal`,
    internal hands its six argument registers on untouched and pushes its own
    stack argument and &amp;gdata above them (`push %rax; lea gdata,%rax; push
    %rax` built -fpie; internal_np, built -fno-pie, pushes `$0x405000`), and
    internal_r reads all six registers and both stack arguments.  A
    single-function decompile printed `internal();` and `internal_np();`:
    passthrough takes a callee that stated nothing by what its body takes, and
    internal reads nothing before its call, so what it takes is what
    internal_r takes, and a run of the whole register file is no arity claim
    (a variadic register save reads the same).  The decline was lifted only at
    the decompiled function's own call, never at internal's call to
    internal_r, which hands internal_r a stack argument and so a full register
    file.  decompile-all, which states internal_r before internal, printed all
    seven arguments.

    twice_pid is `sub $8,%rsp; call pid_plus` and pid_plus is `push %rbx; mov
    %rdi,%rbx; mov $39,%edi; call sys1` with sys1 a syscall() body that reads
    every argument register: the pushed rbx is a save in the slot of sys1's
    first stack argument, no argument, so pid_plus takes only its own rdi.

    twice_cnt is `sub $8,%rsp; call cnt_plus` and cnt_plus, hand-assembled in
    the shape clang -fstack-protector gives a small frame, is `push %rax; mov
    %fs:0x28,%rax; mov %rax,(%rsp); lea (%rdi,%rdi,2),%eax; add
    %eax,0x405000; xor %eax,%eax; call sys1`: the canary is a register stored
    as it stands (through the temporary a 64-bit move copies it into) in
    sys1's first stack-argument slot, no argument, so cnt_plus takes only rdi.

    Stage: P4 active input-trial scoring (kuna_passthrough.rs, option
    passthrough, on by default) on the callee probe's through-call reads
    (kuna_calleedeadarg.rs, stackargs.rs).  Pass 1 is the default; pass 2 sets
    passthrough off, where getopt_long's call has no arguments.
-->
